        {
            "id": "263828",
            "user": "64.12.116.67",
            "timestamp": "02:40, 23 August 2006",
            "anon": true,
            "size": "(5,540 bytes)",
            "change": 0,
            "summary": "",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "adjustment",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#  '''Choose a good quality fan.'''  A cheap fan is more problems than it is worth.  Not only will a cheap fan wobble, but a poor-quality fan will not circulate as much air at a given RPM.  Not only does speed dictate how much air is moved, but also blade pitch (angle between blade and horizontal).  Good quality fans boast motors that have more power, so as to allow for greater blade pitch.  Cheap fans, on the other hand, have motors that are not strong enough to handle the air resistance associated with greater blade pitch, requiring the manufacturer to lower the blade pitch to avoid burning out the motor.  Also cheap fans produce a humming noise, which can be a nuissance.  Remember that a good-quality fan will not always be priced unreasonably high.  Even Hunter ceiling fans, which can be found at stores such as Lowes or Home Depot, are of decent quality for reasonable price."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#  '''Choose a good quality fan.'''  A cheap fan is more problems than it is worth.  Not only will a cheap fan wobble, but poor-quality fan will not circulate as much air at a given speed.  Not only does speed dictate how much air is moved, but also blade pitch (angle between blade and horizontal).  Good quality fans boast motors that have more power, so as to allow for greater blade pitch.  Cheap fans, on the other hand, have motors that are not strong enough to handle the air resistance associated with greater blade pitch, requiring the manufacturer to lower the blade pitch to avoid burning out the motor.  Also cheap fans produce a humming noise, which can be a nuissance.  Remember that a good-quality fan will not always be priced unreasonably high.  Even Hunter ceiling fans, which can be found at stores such as Lowes or Home Depot, are of decent quality for reasonable price."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "262787",
            "user": "72.77.20.138",
            "timestamp": "05:28, 22 August 2006",
            "anon": true,
            "size": "(5,540 bytes)",
            "change": 904,
            "summary": "",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Consider various room factors:<ul><li>'''Room Size'''<ul><li>30\" ceiling fan for rooms up to 8' x 10' (small bedrooms, walk-in closets, smaller kitchens)<li>42\" ceiling fan for rooms up to 12' x 12' (medium bedrooms, kitchens, small recreation areas)<li>52\" ceiling fan for rooms up to 18' x 20' (large bedrooms, family rooms, great rooms, dining rooms)</ul><li>'''Ceiling Height'''<ul><li>Low ceiling: Hugger mount or traditional- mount without down rod<li>Standard 8' ceiling: traditional- mount with down rod<li>9' or Higher ceiling: Extended down rod<li>Sloped ceiling: Extended down rod</ul>You will need to check the floor to ceiling height of the blades. Make sure you take into account the distance that the fan hangs from the ceiling. For safety, a minimum height of 7’-9’ is recommended. If your fan does not meet the 7’ recommendation, you can look into a low-ceiling mount. Building codes in your area may reinforce this.<br>For optimum air circulation it is most efficient to have the fan blade 8' to 9' above the floor. For higher ceilings see the chart for recommended down rod length.</ul>",
                    "#'''Think about electricity.'''  Since fans require the same amount of power as most ceiling fixtures, the electrical circuit shouldn’t be overloaded. If the fan includes a light fixture, ensure that the circuit is able to handle both the fan and light. If your circuit will not hold this capacity then a new circuit must be run from the home’s main panel to the fan. If there was not a pre-existing fixture, you will need to create a place to hang the fan. If your home is not wired properly, some fans do have swag wiring that can be plugged into a wall outlet, but ceiling installation is preferred. It is easiest to install ceiling bracing and electrical wiring during new home construction even if you will be installing a fan at a later time.Consulting an electrician is always your best option.<br><br>",
                    "#  '''Choose a good quality fan.'''  A cheap fan is more problems than it is worth.  Not only will a cheap fan wobble, but poor-quality fan will not circulate as much air at a given speed.  Not only does speed dictate how much air is moved, but also blade pitch (angle between blade and horizontal).  Good quality fans boast motors that have more power, so as to allow for greater blade pitch.  Cheap fans, on the other hand, have motors that are not strong enough to handle the air resistance associated with greater blade pitch, requiring the manufacturer to lower the blade pitch to avoid burning out the motor.  Also cheap fans produce a humming noise, which can be a nuissance.  Remember that a good-quality fan will not always be priced unreasonably high.  Even Hunter ceiling fans, which can be found at stores such as Lowes or Home Depot, are of decent quality for reasonable price.",
                    "*Unless the ceiling height does not allow for a traditional-mount fan (with a downrod) to be mounted with the blades at least 7' above the floor, avoid hugger-mount fans or traditional-mount fans without downrods.  Because the blades of such a fan are extremely close to the ceiling, such a fan would not move as much air as traditional-mount fans at any given speed.",
                    "",
                    "== Warnings ==",
                    "*Always mount a ceiling fan with the blades at least 7' above the floor, even if this requires a close-to-ceiling mount.  A ceiling fan hanging too low can potentially cause a hazardous situation.  If someone were to hit his/her head on a rotating ceiling fan, the results would be painful, and potentially land him/her in the hospital if the resulting injury is bad enough.  In this situation, you will potentially be forced to pay any hospital bills.  Also, builder codes in many jurisdictions prohibit ceiling fans from being mounted with the blades less than 7'."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Consider various room factors:<ul><li>'''Room Size.'''<ul><li>30\" ceiling fan for rooms up to 8' x 10' (small bedrooms, walk-in closets, smaller kitchens)<li>42\" ceiling fan for rooms up to 12' x 12' (medium bedrooms, kitchens, small recreation areas)<li>52\" ceiling fan for rooms up to 18' x 20' (large bedrooms, family rooms, great rooms, dining rooms)</ul><li>'''Ceiling Height'''<ul><li>Low ceiling: Hugger mount or combo mount without down rod<li>Standard 8' ceiling: Combo mount with down rod<li>9' or Higher ceiling: Extended down rod<li>Sloped ceiling: Extended down rod</ul>You will need to check the floor to ceiling height of the blades. Make sure you take into account the distance that the fan hangs from the ceiling. For safety, a minimum height of 7’-9’ is recommended. If your fan does not meet the 7’ recommendation, you can look into a low-ceiling mount. Building codes in your area may reinforce this.<br>For optimum air circulation it is most efficient to have the fan blade 8' to 9' above the floor. For higher ceilings see the chart for recommended down rod length.</ul>",
                    "#'''Think about electricity.'''Since fans require the same amount of power as most ceiling fixtures, the electrical circuit shouldn’t be overloaded. If the fan includes a light fixture, ensure that the circuit is able to handle both the fan and light. If your circuit will not hold this capacity then a new circuit must be run from the home’s main panel to the fan. If there was not a pre-existing fixture, you will need to create a place to hang the fan. If your home is not wired properly, some fans do have swag wiring that can be plugged into a wall outlet, but ceiling installation is preferred. It is easiest to install ceiling bracing and electrical wiring during new home construction even if you will be installing a fan at a later time.Consulting an electrician is always your best option.<br><br>",
                    "#  '''Choose a good quality fan.'''A cheap fan is more problems than it is worth.  Not only will a cheap fan wobble, but poor-quality fan will not circulate as much air at a given speed.  Not only does speed dictate how much air is moved, but also blade pitch (angle between blade and horizontal).  Good quality fans boast motors that have more power, so as to allow for greater blade pitch.  Cheap fans, on the other hand, have motors that are not strong enough to handle the air resistance associated with greater blade pitch, requiring the manufacturer to lower the blade pitch to avoid burning out the motor.  Also cheap fans produce a humming noise, which can be a nuissance.  Remember that a good-quality fan will not always be priced unreasonably high.  Even Hunter ceiling fans, which can be found at stores such as Lowes or Home Depot, are of decent quality for reasonable price.  Therefore, do not just purchase the cheapest fan you can find."
                ]
            }
        },
